 <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="overview"><h2 id="overview">01 / Research overview</h2>
 <h3 id="researcher">Researcher and institutional context</h3>
 <div class="author-profile"><div class="author-heading"><span class="author-monogram" aria-hidden="true">AL</span><div><span class="eyebrow">RESEARCHER</span><p class="author-name">Artyom Lazyan</p></div></div><dl><div><dt>Department</dt><dd>Models of Computations and Logical Reasoning Department</dd></div><div><dt>Institute</dt><dd>Institute for Informatics and Automation Problems</dd></div><div><dt>Academy</dt><dd>National Academy of Sciences of the Republic of Armenia</dd></div><div><dt>Specialization</dt><dd>E.13.04 — Mathematical and Software Support of Computing Machines, Complexes, Systems, and Networks</dd></div><div><dt>Research theme</dt><dd>Multi-user software for mission engineering, execution verification, and management of self-organizing UAV swarms</dd></div></dl><p class="source-note">Institutional affiliation: <a href="https://www.iiap.sci.am/department.php?did=2&amp;dt=1&amp;langcode=1">official department staff listing</a>. Name, institute and specialization restored from the original reader. The research theme describes the work; it does not assert a formal title change.</p></div>
 
 <h3 id="abstract">Abstract</h3>
 <p>We developed and evaluated <strong>Verified Edge Coverage and Recovery (VECR)</strong> as a reusable, algorithm-independent method in the SwarmKit agent. VECR assigns a physical obligation to a directed graph edge, checks bounded navigation and qualified sensing evidence, preserves verified work, identifies unverified intervals, and executes bounded local recovery. Command acceptance, arrival, route traversal and survey fulfillment have distinct meanings. The external application retains ownership of graph planning and distributed coordination.</p>
-<p>The completed <strong>v5.1 study</strong>, repeated after endpoint corrections, used the production SDK → local mTLS AgentService → agent runtime → SimBackend path. A noisy sensor estimator produced verifier-facing camera metadata; private simulated acquisitions and motion supplied independent ground truth. We executed <strong>4,200 primary attempts</strong> across 21 conditions and <strong>1,080 sensitivity attempts</strong> across 18 conditions, with both policies assigned to every seed. A separate serial benchmark executed 80 attempts. Every final assignment completed without an infrastructure error; unsuccessful missions remained in the analysis. The preceding v5 campaign is preserved separately and is not pooled with this replication.</p>
-<p>Against coverage-aware full-edge restart B2, targeted VECR recovery reduced modeled distance by <strong>25.3–27.8%</strong> in three midflight conditions with 100/100 true and reported completions per policy per condition. Across 4,880 honest-sensor attempts, neither policy falsely reported fulfillment. All 400 deliberate dishonest-sensor controls falsely fulfilled, exposing the trusted sensor boundary. Persistent unavailable or overly uncertain evidence reduced acceptance, even when the actual survey was complete. These findings establish conditional utility and limitations within a flat, one-dimensional full-strip model. ArduPilot SITL has not been executed for this study.</p>
+<p>The completed <strong>v6 primary campaign</strong> executed <strong>5,940 attempts: 22 conditions × 30 paired seeds × nine policies</strong> through the production SDK → local mTLS AgentService → agent runtime → SimBackend path. A separate 200-attempt campaign compared direct and routed SDK clients. Independent reconstruction checked 516,751 delivered captures and 3,984 physical re-entry dwells. Historical v5.1 results remain available in the reader and are not pooled with v6.</p>
+<p>The stronger comparison separates <strong>coverage tracking, evidence waiting and target conservatism</strong>. Under delayed metadata, waiting reduced travel by 6.227 m versus immediate gap repair (95% paired CI [−6.291, −6.162] m). The derived uncertainty-envelope target E and the simpler waiting gap rule GW were identical there. Under multiple gaps, E traveled <strong>6.240 m farther</strong> than GW (CI [+6.240, +6.240] m at three-decimal precision), with no extra completions. Gap-aware policies reported 30/30 completions where departure resumption and fixed rewind reported 0/30. These are conditional findings, not predetermined support for a preferred policy.</p>
+<p>Across <strong>5,400 honest-sensor attempts</strong>, no policy falsely fulfilled. The <strong>540 dishonest-sensor controls</strong> are reported separately; authentication does not establish physical truth. The technical contribution is an implemented reusable edge-obligation contract, a restricted conditional recovery rule and a reproducible characterization of its advantages and costs. The data do <strong>not</strong> establish that E is the best recovery algorithm or that returning to a departure point is new. ArduPilot qualification is a separate evidence tier in Chapter 15; real camera quality remains unvalidated.</p>
