+<p>Let g be the first missing coordinate, p current projected progress, a the hard re-entry radius, v the physical speed bound, T the maximum time to a qualifying capture, e the maximum declared capture position error and H the minimum uncertainty-free along half-footprint. All captures must genuinely qualify across the whole strip. The first estimated center is at most q + a + vT + e; its accepted left endpoint is at most q + a + vT + 2e − H. Therefore:</p>
+<div class="equation" role="group" aria-label="Conditional recovery target"><div>q* = min(p, g − a − vT − 2e + H), if q* ≥ 0</div><small>Largest on-edge target satisfying the sufficient first-capture start bound</small></div>
+
+<p>Substitution shows that the first accepted interval <strong>begins no later than g</strong>. It need not contain g. Consecutive accepted intervals cannot separate under the additional sufficient condition <strong>vT + 4e ≤ 2H</strong>. Endpoint coverage still needs actual accepted captures. When the target expression is negative, returning to A is an explicitly unguaranteed retry.</p>
+<p>For current coordinate (x,y), straight transit followed by a sweep to B costs √((x−q)²+y²) + L−q. This decreases as feasible q increases, so q* minimizes that restricted cost. It does not optimize real acceleration, energy, obstacles, future failures or arbitrary routes. The measured E policy can—and does—travel farther than a simpler rule that succeeds without enforcing the sufficient worst-case constraint.</p>
+<p>At a currently verified stationary B, a bounded wait lets delayed observations arrive. It never creates coverage or predicts a future capture. Freshness, safety, endpoint validity and the overall deadline remain active. A fixed wait wastes time when missing data will never arrive. See the <a href="assets/evidence/v6/RECOVERY_MODEL_V6.md">complete derivation</a>.</p>
