-<h3 id="analysis-structure">Analysis structure</h3>
-<p>Assignment counts, realized faults, actual trajectories, accepted evidence, reasons and uncertainty have separate records. A positive effect is not a validation prerequisite. Failed experiments retain their outputs; a fix requires a new versioned run. Conclusions can be added only after measured evidence supports them. SITL cannot establish real-camera image quality or field airworthiness.</p>
+<h3 id="reliability-findings-and-limitations">Reliability findings and limitations</h3>
+<p>The retained first pilot exposed port collisions, a Python/C++ monotonic clock-domain mismatch and unsuccessful takeoff handling. Corrected pilots isolate auxiliary ports, check the clock epoch and retain command acknowledgements. Later nominal flights exposed a real backend ordering defect: ArduPilot resets speed when initializing a new GUIDED position submode. SwarmKit now reapplies the requested speed after dispatch; regressions cover acknowledged reposition, unacknowledged setpoint fallback and explicit failure after dispatch. Every unsuccessful pilot remains archived.</p>
+<p>The observation envelope assumes SIM_STATE transport age at receipt ≤50 ms and actual horizontal speed ≤10 m/s. Snapshot age at receipt is monitored, but SIM_STATE carries <strong>no acquisition timestamp</strong>, so the upstream-age assumption is not independently proved. Reconstructed arrival and re-entry use sampled truth; they do not establish continuous-flight dwell. The camera is ideal, route-aligned and independent of vehicle attitude. No real image-quality, terrain, gimbal or airworthiness claim follows. Two flights per cell do not support a broad reliability interval.</p>
+<p>Future calibrated image evidence and physical aircraft tests remain <strong>TBD — not executed</strong>. Their numbers will be added only after actual measurements. The <a href="assets/evidence/v6/SITL_PROTOCOL_V6.md">SITL protocol</a> defines the evidence boundary.</p>
 </section><section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="conclusion"><h2 id="conclusion">16 / Conclusion and references</h2>
 <p>We developed VECR in SwarmKit and completed its defined agent-backed digital evaluation. The method connects graph-edge execution to navigation and sensing evidence, retains completed intervals and recovers locally from missing work. Its measured benefit is lower recovery travel in the tested equal-success conditions. Its measured weaknesses are evidence-driven nonacceptance and dependence on truthful sensor assertions.</p>
-<p>The defined simulator study, statistics, sensitivity analysis, benchmark and independent reconstruction are complete. <strong>ArduPilot SITL remains unexecuted.</strong> The literature review additionally identifies unfinished novelty substantiation and stronger comparative evaluation before a defense-readiness claim. Hardware, real camera calibration, complex terrain, obstacle/geofence-aware recovery, remote/multi-UAV campaigns, OS power-loss experiments and cloud deployment lie outside the completed simulator claim. These boundaries remain relevant after SITL.</p>
+<p>The completed v6 campaign extends the retained v5.1 study with stronger comparators, acquisition-time evidence handling, a conditional sensing-envelope rule and API reuse. Its primary finding is qualified: evidence-aware gap tracking and bounded waiting are useful in the declared conditions, while the conservative E target has no measured completion advantage over GW and often costs more travel. The complete records include negative outcomes, unsuccessful pilots and a corrected protocol deviation.</p>
+<p>ArduPilot qualification is reported separately from SimBackend and from real aircraft. Native accuracy remains unqualified for hard-bound acceptance; the positive integration tier uses explicitly synthetic navigation and camera observations. Hardware, calibrated image quality, complex terrain, obstacle/geofence-aware recovery, remote/multi-UAV campaigns and operational fleet reliability remain outside the established evidence. A targeted literature comparison supports the narrow systems contribution; it does not certify global novelty or degree acceptance.</p>
